WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:09.000
Why does Route 66 matter?

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:21.140
“Hi. My name is Jean and I’m from France.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:24.600
And last year I went on a trip in the West
Coast, and

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:27.400
we passed by Route 66.”

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:32.140
“Our trip was from LA to Chicago all the

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:37.820
way, taking the mother road, haha.”
“We would stop at all the museums on the

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:43.200
way, we stopped at the one in Elk City, Oklahoma
and we stood on top of the train.”

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:49.780
“I’m Fabian from Germany. Last year, I
was visiting my family in California.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:54.620
While driving on Route 66, I had to stop to take photos of the beautiful sunset.”

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:59.680
“The plan of the trip is to have no plan
at all.”

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:12.460
“My husband and I went back and even visited
Route 66 and Williams for our honeymoon.”

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:20.940
“Hey.”

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:27.100
Why is Route 66 not only famous, but internationally

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:30.640
famous?
“Starting off Route 66.”

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:36.799
The road starts in Chicago, slides down
the country,

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:38.880
and ends up all the way in Santa Monica.

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:41.600
Convert that distance to time and you get

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:45.800
a different story.
In 1926, the road was commissioned.

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:53.630
By 1957, the Interstate Highway System began,
and it bypassed the route by 1970. In 1985,

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:58.939
Route 66 was fully decommissioned.
Route 66 has been in the shadows twice as

00:01:58.939 --> 00:02:03.429
long as it was in the spotlight.
But there’s still this energy around it.

00:02:03.429 --> 00:02:08.959
I talked to Ron Warnick, he’s the editor
of Route 66 News, which is an obsessive Route

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:16.360
66 site, and his articles just came alive
with people reminiscing about Route 66.

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:23.560
“It was about Oklahoma Joes. It was this
dive bar in Albuquerque near the University

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:28.659
of New Mexico campus. I put it out there,
and pretty soon, all sorts of people were

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:32.500
exchanging their memories about the bar.”

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:43.760
This road has three distinct eras.

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:48.440
It’s got secrets, and surprises, and even a future.

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:59.160
The Big Texan Steak Ranch in Amarillo has

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:06.930
a challenge: eat 72 ounces of steak and sides,
in under an hour, and you get it for free.

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:14.290
I am going to switch to phone mode here. Alright.
Before you get to a cardiac arrest-threatening challenge

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:19.200
like this, you actually have to go back more
than a hundred years.

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:26.140
Before Federal highways, networks of largely
privately owned auto trails, like the ones

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:33.639
on this 1920 Rand McNally map, were standard.
Look at the chaotic number of options in the

00:03:33.639 --> 00:03:36.719
legend.
As Federal highway funding laws were passed

00:03:36.719 --> 00:03:43.420
in the 1910s and 1920s, new maps planned a
linked highway system, like this one drafted

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:50.250
by World War I General John Pershing.
This telegram from April 30, 1926, from Springfield,

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:56.709
Missouri established Route 66 (they initially
wanted the nice round number of Route 60,

00:03:56.709 --> 00:04:00.849
but settled for 66).
Cyrus Avery is called the “father of Route

00:04:00.849 --> 00:04:07.060
66” for helping create the highway to promote
his home of Tulsa and creating the U.S. Highway

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:12.159
66 Association the next year.
That connection from Chicago to Santa Monica

00:04:12.159 --> 00:04:18.410
was always a weird shape, and less intuitive
than a transcontinental road. But it had lobbying

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:22.650
interest behind it and a good starting point
with existing roads.

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:28.620
Texaco rated road conditions in maps like
this 1934 one. As the legend shows, Route

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:34.990
66 was just a graded road in parts, basically
flattened dirt. Look at the journey from Amarillo

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:40.210
to Glenrio. There’s still parts of Route
66 that look like this today. But they finished

00:04:40.210 --> 00:04:45.009
paving the whole thing in 1937.
To get all that work started in the 1920s,

00:04:45.009 --> 00:04:50.650
the Route 66 association pushed stunts
and did publicity that wouldn’t have seemed

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:55.780
out of place in the 1950s and 60s.
When a transcontinental footrace called the

00:04:55.780 --> 00:05:01.770
“Bunion Derby” was run, the association
made sure a big part of it took place on Route 66.

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:05.370
But it was struggle that initially made Route

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:08.080
66’s reputation.

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:13.840
“We’re going to California, ain’t we? Alright then, let’s go to California.”

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:17.860
The Great Depression and Dust Bowl — a rut

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:22.560
of drought and erosion — sent families looking
West for a better life.

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:27.530
Route 66 was perfectly designed to scoop them
up, leading John Steinbeck to write that these

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:33.050
migrants “come into 66 from the tributary
side roads, from the wagon tracks and the

00:05:33.050 --> 00:05:38.280
rutted country roads. 66 is the mother road,
the road of flight.”

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:44.610
Though records show that highways 60 and 70
actually admitted more traffic to California,

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:50.000
Route 66 had become the iconic “mother road.”
And then things really got going.

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:56.560
Okay, I have a slight wait before I dine alone,
so there’s time for activities.

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:00.660
That thing’s terrifying. “Oh, it is.”

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:08.540
This boot’s a metaphor for steak — and boots.

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:12.480
It’s time.

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:20.660
I feel like the only way
to go mega-vi is to get that 72 ounce steak.

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:29.220
OK, see that big 66 on there?
Amarillo, Texas, is a good example of a big

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:32.930
Route 66 town.
They were already a transportation center.

00:06:32.930 --> 00:06:39.470
This 1926 map — the year Route 66 started
— shows Amarillo was a railroad hub.

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:45.889
After World War II ended, that existing commerce
and Route 66 made it easy to add roadside

00:06:45.889 --> 00:06:48.400
attractions.
And it’s still that way today.

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:59.610
Over time, Route 66 did this for towns a lot
smaller than Amarillo, too.

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:04.520
This is the middle of the video, by the way.
Right now. Yeah.

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:06.580
It also did it for Vega, Texas.

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:16.720
Carolyn was nice enough to be my tour guide.

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:18.100
She showed me her house.

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:26.940
“This is Ben’s dad. And grandpa and me.”

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:28.940
“Oh wow.”

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:35.080
She’s very into dinosaurs.

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:39.160
“Armorage and spikes. And teeth.”

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:42.800
“And so where did you find these?”

00:08:42.820 --> 00:08:46.720
“Out North of Town.”
And she also showed me the Magnolia Gas Station,

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:51.760
which got started just before Route 66 became
official. She actually helped restore the

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:54.960
space, including the second floor, where people
used to live.

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:04.840
“OK.”
“It’s a filling station, but somebody

00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:09.440
said at one time they also sold ice. I’m
not too sure about that part.”

00:09:14.860 --> 00:09:23.700
“The kitchen was green. The bedroom was
blue. See, I hung pans up there so you’d

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:24.960
know it was the kitchen.”

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:29.840
“Those pictures are neat because they show

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:34.140
the horses pulling the cars out of the water.”

00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:43.040
To support all that travel and all those attractions,

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:49.800
Route 66 had a unique motel culture.
Of course, even as it succeeded, Route 66

00:09:49.810 --> 00:09:53.090
was limited by the prevailing prejudices of
the time.

00:09:53.090 --> 00:09:57.300
The Green Book was a traveling guide for black
motorists to find safe lodging.

00:09:57.300 --> 00:10:02.250
In Tucumcari, New Mexico, in 1960, listed options
were scarce.

00:10:02.250 --> 00:10:06.350
Route 66 made a culture, but it didn’t change
the existing one.

00:10:06.350 --> 00:10:13.560
And in the 1960s, just as the Route 66 road
trip hit its peak, the road was already being eclipsed.

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:36.000
Today, Glenrio, New Mexico is a ghost town.

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:38.520
It’s not alone.

00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:47.760
After the Interstate Highway Act of 1956,

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:52.560
new, better funded interstates were built
for defense and infrastructure.

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:58.690
In Texas and New Mexico, you can see how I-40
followed Route 66 in some spots, but also

00:10:58.690 --> 00:11:02.250
split away.
This is what that can look like.

00:11:02.250 --> 00:11:04.300
But it doesn’t have to.

00:11:04.940 --> 00:11:06.940
“We had the privilege of designing and creating

00:11:06.940 --> 00:11:09.880
two murals, one here in Joplin, Missouri, and
another in Galena, Kansas.”

00:11:09.880 --> 00:11:14.600
“We wanted to help revitalize and show off
our local area on historic Route 66.”

00:11:14.600 --> 00:11:16.080
“Route 66.”

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:18.320
“I’m the president of the Oklahoma Statewide

00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:24.120
Route 66 association and I’m the chair of
the Tulsa Route 66 commission. About ten years

00:11:24.120 --> 00:11:27.670
ago, I sold everything I owned and left the
country and I backpacked for ten months throughout

00:11:27.670 --> 00:11:32.260
Southeast Asia and Europe. When I got home
to Tulsa, which is where I was raised, I thought

00:11:32.260 --> 00:11:36.400
Okay, I’ve seen all these amazing places,
what does Tulsa have? Of course, Route 66

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:39.560
goes right through Tulsa. I thought, well,
it’s been here the whole time, I haven’t

00:11:39.560 --> 00:11:41.700
really paid attention, and started exploring
it.”

00:11:57.900 --> 00:12:03.140
“I’m Larry Smith and I’m owner/operator
of the Motel Safari in Tucumcari, New Mexico,

00:12:03.140 --> 00:12:07.740
on historic Route 66.”
“Yeah, this is the motel I’m staying at.”

00:12:13.020 --> 00:12:18.040
“I’d hit a wall with my job at the time
and I noticed while I was driving 66 that

00:12:18.050 --> 00:12:25.630
the road was using a lot of that older generation.
It really needed the right people to own the

00:12:25.630 --> 00:12:28.580
businesses along the Route.”

00:12:28.600 --> 00:12:31.660
But saving Route 66 doesn’t answer the big

00:12:31.660 --> 00:12:35.500
question: why it matters.

00:12:36.820 --> 00:12:41.460
So I did not eat a 72-ounce steak. But I found

00:12:41.500 --> 00:12:46.280
somebody who did.
“This is the most excited I’ve been for

00:12:46.290 --> 00:12:54.010
an interview since I talked to an astronaut.”
“The story behind it is that I compete in

00:12:54.010 --> 00:12:58.420
track and field professionally, I throw the
shot. I was kind of injured at the time and

00:12:58.420 --> 00:13:03.490
I performed really terribly and we were gonna
drive through Amarillo. I heard about the

00:13:03.490 --> 00:13:08.570
steak, so I’m like, ‘I have to have one
win.’ And honestly, it’s not the fullness,

00:13:08.570 --> 00:13:15.430
it’s the chewing. By the end I was like,
I can’t chew anything else and I was drinking

00:13:15.430 --> 00:13:20.150
as much water as possible to get it down.
I’m glad I did it but I don’t know if

00:13:20.150 --> 00:13:25.610
I’d ever do that again. I mean, I do a lot
of ridiculous things. Hey, didn’t do well

00:13:25.610 --> 00:13:31.870
at this track meet? I’m gonna eat a 72-ounce
steak to prove to myself I can overcome something.”

00:13:31.870 --> 00:13:38.260
The Big Texan Ranch isn’t on Route 66 anymore.
The owner moved it closer to I-40 in 1970.

00:13:38.260 --> 00:13:45.410
And yet it still is Route 66.
We think of places on a map as dots. But maybe

00:13:45.410 --> 00:13:47.400
a place can be a line.

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:07.260
“There was desert as far as the eye can

00:14:07.260 --> 00:14:11.060
see.”
“I was getting to the point where I needed

00:14:11.070 --> 00:14:16.523
a break from seeing patients in and out. So
I called up my friend from med school and

00:14:16.523 --> 00:14:20.300
said, ‘hey we have this window, would you
be interested in a road trip?’”

00:14:22.120 --> 00:14:27.420
“Route 66 kinda became a character in our
journey. It was kinda like the Oregon Trail

00:14:27.430 --> 00:14:32.771
with all the challenges popping up, and the
prize at the end was our new home at the end

00:14:32.771 --> 00:14:36.340
of the highway.”
“I had a flat tire. So I took my camera

00:14:36.340 --> 00:14:41.190
out and took some long exposure shots of
my car and the night sky.”

00:14:41.190 --> 00:14:49.002
“So the graduation gift to my three boys
as they exit high school is a 14-day

00:14:49.002 --> 00:14:55.410
driving trip out West — St. George, Utah
— to meet my biological family.”

00:14:55.410 --> 00:15:03.920
“My great great grandfather, Ramon Negrette,
emigrated from Mexico to a tiny town in Arizona

00:15:03.920 --> 00:15:10.730
called Williams in the early 1900s, before Route
66 was there. He painted the house yellow

00:15:10.730 --> 00:15:17.520
and it is still there today, the yellow house
in Williams. We think my great grandma still haunts

00:15:17.520 --> 00:15:24.900
the house? What I love about Route 66 is that
it’s not just a road that’s going through

00:15:24.910 --> 00:15:33.300
tiny towns and big towns in America. It’s
a road that goes through people’s histories

00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:40.660
and carries legacies of perseverance and hope,
and I think that’s what makes it so fascinating

00:15:40.660 --> 00:15:41.660
and so beautiful.”

00:16:04.660 --> 00:16:12.180
Alright, that’s it for this road trip along
Route 66. I’m about to read a couple of

00:16:12.190 --> 00:16:17.990
comments from the last episode all about why
every suburb looks the same, but first I just

00:16:17.990 --> 00:16:23.320
want to give a little plug for the Vox Video
Lab. In there right now I’ve got a special

00:16:23.320 --> 00:16:31.300
video that shows exactly how I did one shot
in the Route 66 video that you just saw. It’s

00:16:31.300 --> 00:16:35.810
an obsessive, nerdy, technical breakdown and
that’s the kind of behind-the-scenes stuff

00:16:35.810 --> 00:16:41.050
that you always get in the Video Lab, in addition
to supporting big videos like this one. Now

00:16:41.050 --> 00:16:45.010
let’s look at a couple of comments.
“As a European, it’s so weird to see streets

00:16:45.010 --> 00:16:50.000
without a pavement/sidewalk. Where tf are
you supposed to walk?”

00:16:50.000 --> 00:16:53.140
Yeah.
City Beautiful and Vox both made a video on

00:16:53.140 --> 00:16:57.990
this in the same day.
Yes. This is the craziest coincidence I’ve

00:16:57.990 --> 00:17:03.450
experienced in almost 100 videos. We turned
out to be video soulmates and we made this

00:17:03.450 --> 00:17:08.250
video very very close apart even though both
of us had been working on it for months. But

00:17:08.250 --> 00:17:13.279
the take home point here, besides a crazy
coincidence or glitch in the Matrix? City

00:17:13.279 --> 00:17:18.129
Beautiful’s an awesome channel if you’re
interested in urban planning. Go ahead and

00:17:18.129 --> 00:17:22.470
add them to your subscription feed if you
want more videos like that. That’s it for

00:17:22.470 --> 00:17:22.960
this one, the next episode of this Road Trip
edition of Almanac is the last one, and it

00:17:22.960 --> 00:17:28.240
tackles how roads can shape public policy
in really unexpected ways.

